The loop that binds a thread to its units claims a bound of two,
and a thread may be bound to more units than that,
so the claim is false as soon as one is.
The code is the same, so only the counting harness catches it.

Caught-by: fuzz-work
Report: the loop at kernel/sched.c ran N times on one entry, beyond its bound of N
Witness: fuzz-work actor-handoff

diff --git a/kernel/sched.c b/kernel/sched.c
--- a/kernel/sched.c
+++ b/kernel/sched.c
@@ -513,7 +513,7 @@ void sched_bind(struct place *p, uint32_t first, uint32_t count, uint8_t rights,
     *b = (struct cap){ .type = CAP_BOUND, .rights = rights, .index = p->index, .a = first, .b = count };
     cap_attach(parent, b);
     for (uint32_t i = 0; i < count; i++) {
-        LOOP_BOUND(TIME_UNITS);
+        LOOP_BOUND(2);
         unit_place[first + i] = v2p(p);
